TITLE:Fault Tables
H1:The fault tables: what can go wrong with each machine <span class="draft">for approval</span>
SUB:For each kind of machine, its causes in plain words: what's wrong, what the job card says, what you see up close, when it shows, and what you hear when the wrong part goes in. Real faults (from Day 2) pick their cause from here; the scanner (Day 5) reads these parts; head-scratchers pick their suspects and clues from here. Tier 1 kinds first and complete; Tier 2 kinds drafted for Step 3. Data for section 17's <code>FAULT_CAUSES</code> (foundation 17.4).
NAV:plain=In plain words|rules=How to read them|t1=Tier 1|t2=Tier 2 (draft)|b=Builder|check=Checks|faq=FAQ

<p class="note"><b>Approved by the lead (2026-10-18, overnight authority).</b></p>
<section id="plain">
  <h2>In plain words</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A machine doesn't just "break": something in it goes. An oven runs cool because its element is failing, or because its door seal lets the heat out. The <b>job card</b> says what everyone notices ("runs cool"); <b>up close</b> you can tell more ("cool all over" or "cool by the door").</li>
    <li>From Day 2 every real fault has one of these causes. Before the scanner, inspecting tells you the part, as now. With the scanner you read the parts and choose. In a head-scratcher, the other causes become the suspects.</li>
    <li>A few causes are <b>settings</b> (fixed by tuning, M5), and a few are <b>loads</b> (the machine is fine; something else is pulling on it), which only come up as head-scratchers from your Officer days.</li>
    <li>Every part named here is already on a shelf in main storage. Nothing new to stock.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="rules">
  <h2>How to read the tables</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>How common</b> (<code>w</code>): ●●● often, ●● sometimes, ● rarely. A worn part makes its cause likelier still.</li>
    <li><b>Card</b> (<code>shows</code>): the symptom on the job card. Causes that share it can be suspects together (P3 needs at least three with the same card).</li>
    <li><b>Up close</b> (<code>sign</code>): what you see at the machine, on rounds or the look-over. Causes with the same card have different signs, so when the scanner shows two low parts, the sign tells them apart.</li>
    <li><b>When</b>: always, at peak (its busy hours), warm (after it's been on a while), after use, touched (when someone uses the control), or at a time.</li>
    <li><b>Still</b>: the machine's line when you fit the wrong part.</li>
    <li><b>From</b>: the first band it can appear in (Tier 1 machines in T1 from Day 2; "Officer" means only in Tier 2 bands and later).</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="t1">
  <h2>Tier 1 machines (complete)</h2>

  <h3>Oven <span class="tag">oven · Deck 3 galley · part: element</span></h3>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <tr><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th><th>Card</th><th>Up close</th><th>When</th><th>How common</th><th>Still (wrong fit)</th><th>From</th></tr>
    <tr><td>The element is failing</td><td>element</td><td>runs cool</td><td>cool all over, slow to heat</td><td>always</td><td>●●●</td><td>"New element in. Still cool by the door."</td><td>Day 2</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The door seal is worn</td><td>seal</td><td>runs cool</td><td>cool by the door; you feel heat at the gap</td><td>always</td><td>●●</td><td>"Fresh seal. Still slow to heat all over."</td><td>Day 2</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The fan's belt is slipping</td><td>fan belt</td><td>runs cool</td><td>hot at the back, cool at the front; a squeal at start</td><td>after use</td><td>●</td><td>"New belt. Still cool."</td><td>Day 2</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Its setting has drifted</td><td>tune (M5)</td><td>runs hot / runs cool</td><td>the glow stutters (hot) or the screen dims (cool)</td><td>always</td><td>●●</td><td>—</td><td>Day 4</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The galley's power junction sags under load</td><td>load: the Deck 3 junction (Tier 3, not yours)</td><td>runs cool</td><td>fine at breakfast, cool at dinner</td><td>at peak (with the range and extractor)</td><td>●</td><td>—</td><td>Officer (P1)</td></tr>
  </table></div>

